	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Assembled components vs a single binary</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu doesn't implement mail protocols itself: it wraps Postfix, Dovecot and Rspamd,
			each maintained as its own upstream project, in purpose-built containers behind
			an nginx front end, with Redis for state. Stalwart implements SMTP, IMAP, POP3, JMAP
			and the DAV protocols itself, in Rust, as a single binary with a published security
			audit. Version 1.0 shipped in October 2026 with a new database layout that existing
			v0.16 servers have to migrate to.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Setup and footprint</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu's documented minimum is 1 GB of RAM plus
			1 GB of swap without antivirus, or 3 GB with ClamAV enabled. You configure it through
			a web setup wizard that generates a <code>docker-compose.yml</code> and a
			<code>mailu.env</code>, choosing optional pieces such as antivirus, webmail, CalDAV and
			full-text search. Stalwart 1.0 no longer quotes a minimum: its memory use is set by cache
			limits that scale with the host, and on a 2 GiB host the defaults come to about 1.4 GiB,
			excluding large-account caches that stay empty on small servers. It installs as a single binary on Linux, macOS, FreeBSD or Windows, or
			as a Docker container, and is configured from its web admin.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Protocols, webmail and groupware</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart implements JMAP for mail, calendars and contacts, plus CalDAV, CardDAV and
			WebDAV, all built in. Mailu covers SMTP, IMAP and POP3, and
			adds calendar and contacts through an optional Radicale container.
		</p>
		<p>
			Mailu bundles a webmail, Roundcube or SnappyMail. Stalwart's web interface is for administration and
			account self-service, not reading mail, so you add a webmail yourself or rely on
			desktop and mobile clients. Neither speaks Exchange ActiveSync.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Filtering</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu ships Rspamd with autolearn, greylisting and DMARC/SPF checks, plus optional ClamAV
			and oletools for malicious-attachment scanning. Stalwart has its own built-in spam
			filter with a statistical classifier, Pyzor and spam traps (LLM analysis is an
			Enterprise feature); antivirus means running ClamAV yourself and connecting it as a
			milter.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Scale, identity and APIs</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart supports scaling out: native clustering, Kubernetes or Swarm, pluggable
			storage (RocksDB, PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, FoundationDB, S3-compatible, Azure), and
			LDAP, OIDC or SQL directories. Read replicas, sharded storage, SCIM and multi-tenancy
			are Enterprise features. Mailu is a single-host Compose stack; a community Helm chart
			exists for Kubernetes, though its own docs say it is looking for maintainers. Both have
			an admin API: Mailu's REST API covers everything its admin UI does, with a built-in
			Swagger UI.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Upgrades</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu upgrades by checking the changelog for compose and environment changes, then
			pulling new images and recreating the containers. Stalwart now follows semantic
			versioning: patch releases need no review, but minor releases can change behaviour, so
			its guide says to read the notes and back up the data stores first. Moving an existing
			v0.16 server to 1.0 is a one-off migration with the <code>stalwart-migrate</code> tool,
			not an in-place upgrade. Neither takes a pre-update snapshot, runs a
			health-check gate or rolls back on failure.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Licensing and cost</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu is MIT-licensed throughout, with no paid edition. Stalwart is dual-licensed: the
			AGPL-3.0 core is free, and a proprietary Enterprise edition, priced per mailbox, adds
			multi-tenancy and branding, SCIM provisioning, AI-assisted spam filtering, archiving and
			mail undelete, masked email, read replicas and sharded storage, and live telemetry, with
			premium support included from 150 mailboxes. Since October 2026 Stalwart develops new
			features and keeps its test suites in private repositories; the complete source of every
			release is still published under the AGPL-3.0.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Programmatic sending</h3>
		<p>
			Neither is a transactional sending platform. Mailu's API manages configuration, and
			applications send through SMTP submission as a real mailbox. Stalwart's JMAP can submit
			mail and its webhooks report events, but your application integrates against a mail
			protocol. There are no domain-scoped sending keys, per-domain rate limits or delivery
			analytics in either.
		</p>
	</section>


	<section class="section" id="vectis-mail" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The third option: Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			If what's missing from <em>both</em> is a transactional sending API, or an update you
			don't have to supervise, that's the gap Vectis Mail is built to close. It runs the same
			proven Postfix and Dovecot core as Mailu, with Rspamd by default, installed by one
			guided command:
		</p>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				<li>Runs on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS from 2 GB of RAM without ClamAV (4 GB recommended), in 10 containers by default.</li>
				<li>Domains, mailboxes, aliases and API keys managed from an admin dashboard or the REST API, taking effect immediately with no restarts.</li>
				<li>A 40+ endpoint REST API: transactional sending (single and batch, with attachments), HMAC-signed inbound webhooks, domain-scoped API keys with per-domain rate limits, and analytics.</li>
				<li>Atomic 6-phase updates (snapshot, migrate, pull, deploy, health-check, complete) with automatic rollback, and <code>vectis verify</code> to prove the box runs exactly what was published.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
			Vectis Mail's scope: it's Docker-only and single-node, and doesn't ship JMAP,
			calendar, contacts or ActiveSync. See
			the dedicated <a href="/alternatives/stalwart/">Vectis Mail vs Stalwart</a> comparison,
			and the <a href="/guides/best-self-hosted-email-servers-2026/">best self-hosted email
			servers guide</a> for where Mailu fits in the wider landscape.
		</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail free</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View pricing</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>What's the difference between Mailu and Stalwart?</h3>
				<p>
					Mailu packages Postfix, Dovecot and Rspamd as a Docker Compose stack with webmail,
					an admin UI and a REST API, configured through a web setup wizard and MIT-licensed.
					Stalwart implements every protocol itself in one Rust binary, with JMAP,
					CalDAV/CardDAV/WebDAV and clustering built in and no bundled webmail; its core is
					AGPL-3.0 with a proprietary Enterprise edition, and it reached version 1.0 in
					October 2026.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How much RAM do Mailu and Stalwart need?</h3>
				<p>
					Mailu's documented minimum is 1 GB of RAM plus 1 GB of swap without antivirus, or 3
					GB plus 1 GB of swap with ClamAV. Stalwart 1.0's documentation no longer quotes a
					minimum: memory use is set by cache limits that scale with the host and fill only as
					accounts are used. On a 2 GiB host its default limits add up to about 1.4 GiB, not
					counting two large-account caches that stay empty on small servers, and each limit
					can be lowered. Antivirus on Stalwart means running ClamAV separately as a milter,
					which needs its own memory.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Do Mailu and Stalwart support calendar and contacts?</h3>
				<p>
					Both can. Stalwart has CalDAV, CardDAV and WebDAV built in, plus JMAP for
					calendars and contacts. Mailu offers calendar and contacts through an optional
					Radicale container you enable at setup. Neither supports Exchange ActiveSync.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Stalwart production-ready?</h3>
				<p>
					Stalwart reached version 1.0 on 26 October 2026 (v1.0.1 followed on 28 October) and
					now follows semantic versioning: its upgrade guide says patch releases need no
					further review, while minor releases can change behaviour, so read their notes and
					back up the data stores first. Existing v0.16 servers can't upgrade in place: 1.0
					uses a new database layout, and data moves across with the stalwart-migrate tool,
					which copies while 0.16 keeps serving and needs downtime only for the final pass.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does either have a transactional sending API?</h3>
				<p>
					Not in the SendGrid sense. Mailu's REST API manages configuration (domains,
					users, aliases), and applications send through SMTP submission as a real
					mailbox. Stalwart offers JMAP, which can submit mail, plus event webhooks.
					Neither offers domain-scoped sending keys, per-domain rate limits or delivery
					analytics. Vectis Mail is a self-hosted platform built to add that sending API
					alongside mailbox hosting.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Stalwart support ActiveSync?</h3>
				<p>
					No. Stalwart syncs mail over IMAP, POP3 and JMAP, and calendars and contacts over
					CalDAV, CardDAV and JMAP, but it doesn't implement Exchange ActiveSync, and neither
					does Mailu. Phones and desktop apps connect over IMAP plus CalDAV/CardDAV instead:
					iOS and macOS support both natively, while Android usually needs a DAV sync app such
					as DAVx5.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Can Stalwart replace Dovecot and Postfix?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes, that is its design. Stalwart implements SMTP (Postfix's job) and IMAP and POP3
					(Dovecot's job), plus JMAP and the DAV protocols, in one Rust process, with its own
					spam filter in place of Rspamd. Mailu takes the other route: it runs the real
					Postfix, Dovecot and Rspamd in separate containers. The trade-off is one modern
					binary to run versus components with decades of production history.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>What do you need to self-host Mailu?</h3>
				<p>
					A Linux server with Docker and Docker Compose, at least 1 GB of RAM plus 1 GB of
					swap (3 GB plus swap with ClamAV), a domain whose DNS you control, and a host where
					port 25 is open in both directions, which many home connections and some cloud
					providers block. Mailu's web setup wizard generates the docker-compose.yml and
					mailu.env; after starting the stack, you publish the MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC records
					its admin interface shows for each domain.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

